A1. Pen name. ~ NUMBER
A6. This washes up most Bull Fighters. ~ CASTILE
A12. A tearful bulb. ~ ONION
A13. Plant. ~ FACTORY
A15. This is the end that shapes our destiny. ~ TINY
A16. Cauliflower. ~ EARMARK
A17. The beginning and end of the name of an unknown Mr. ~ SO
A18. It is quite a trick to make this kind of a dog stand. ~ HOT
A19. Eyefuls. ~ CINDERS
A20. "Operator 13." ~ SPY
A21. This almost begins finish, and finishes begin. ~ IN
A22. When these come from jugs, they give jigs and jogs. ~ JAGS
A23. Abbreviated thoroughfare. ~ ST
A24. This will make any chicken scram. ~ SHOO
A25. When the old Romans had a toot on, this is what it was called. ~ GOTH
A26. There is a hidden meaning for this. ~ LATENT
A28. When he threw red. ~ GARNET
A30. Don't let this puzzle do this to you. ~ ENRAGE
A31. You might get a little dough for this, or at least one buck. ~ DEER
A32. It will be necessary to go deep for this. ~ DIVE
A33. Because the building had so many of these broken, the tenants were very much put out. ~ LEASES
A35. Those who can pay their debts and will not. ~ PIKERS
A37. What the comic strip section is to the Sunday newspaper. ~ INSERT
A38. If this is fitted to a "T," it will help you along, but with an "M" you will never get through. (plur.) ~ IRES
A39. Something a good cook always does. ~ GOES
A40. This will dawn on you before noon tomorrow. ~ AM
A42. One thing a lawyer does when he goes to court. ~ WOOS
A43. Don't forget to write, kind Vert. 13 to the author. ~ PS
A44. What dad's salary was before he was married. ~ HIS
A45. "Even as you or I." ~ PRONOUN
A47. What must be done to control those "wild, wild waves." ~ SET
A48. Tax Drive (abbr.) ~ TD
A49. Jittery lane. (plur.) ~ DETOURS
A50. The kind of pudding Sandy MacTavish and his bride had for their first meal. ~ RICE
A51. Some tourists gobi here on account of the camels and sandwiches near. (plur.) ~ DESERTS
A52. This will bite on almost anything. ~ MOLAR
A53. President Roosevelt believes that this is a growing need of the country. ~ REFORESTATION

D1. [missing] ~ NOTHING
D2. The suit that was tried without witnesses. ~ UNION
D3. Under the new administration, there has been more change made here than any other place. ~ MINT
D4. The kind of cots they use in China. ~ BOY
D5. Make yourself a salad by combining this with Hor. No. 35. ~ EN
D6. An artist drew this and received a high honor. ~ CARD
D7. Climaxes. ~ ACMES
D8. Young moderns like to do this to their elders. ~ STARTLE
D9. The last of the visitors. ~ TORS
D10. To annoy a noisy oyster. ~ IRK
D11. This ends happily. ~ LY
D13. These are always going around blowing. ~ FANS
D14. "She comes howlin' round the mountain, when she comes." (But she lives on the prairie) ~ COYOTE
D16. Two two two two. ~ EIGHT
D17. Here's the man who does not pay and pay. ~ SPONGER
D19. The fellow who is always making provisions for banquets. ~ CATERER
D20. These will certainly be shocked this fall. ~ SHEAVES
D22. These are hard people to keep up with. ~ JONESES
D24. What the clock does for shorter hours. ~ STRIKES
D25. Palm oil (pl.) ~ GREASES
D27. Log holder. ~ ANDIRON
D29. This should have its coming-out party at an early age. ~ ADENOID
D33. These travel very very fast, and yet hold up the traffic. ~ LIGHTS
D34. Here is one that will get you going. ~ STARTER
D35. Holy mackerel must be this kind of fish. ~ PIOUS
D36. The latest thing in pants. ~ SISTER
D41. A star Mary Pickford used to owen. ~ MOORE
D42. This is yet to come. ~ WORST
D43. Tough nut. ~ PECAN
D45. Cuban buck. ~ PESO
D46. Where Uncle Walt goes when he wishes to read his paper and the radio is turned on. ~ NUTS
D47. Everyone makes room for fodder here. ~ SILO
D49. A brief definition. ~ DEF
D50. A French king. Any Louis will do. ~ ROI
D51. This one is always in debt. ~ DE
D52. This stands for something big, but you will find it empty. ~ MT


